.TH PROCESS-REPORT 1 "2026-10-14" "SASD Freeware" "Unix Administration"
.SH NAME
process-report \- read-only current-process inventory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B process-report
[--user NAME] [--state LETTER] [--limit 1..5000] [--input FILE] [--null]
.br
.B process-report
--help | --version
.SH DESCRIPTION
Uses native ps with fields PID, PPID, USER, STATE, ELAPSED and COMMAND.
This is a snapshot, not historical process or authentication evidence.
The COMMAND field corresponds to ps comm, not a full command line.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B --user NAME
Show processes for one user.
.TP
.B --state LETTER
Filter by the first letter of the process state (for example R, S or Z).
.TP
.B --limit N
Show at most N rows (default 200, maximum 5000); Matching reports all matches.
.TP
.B --input FILE
Parse saved headerless ps output, refusing symbolic links.
.TP
.B --null
Output six raw NUL-terminated fields per row, no header or totals.
.TP
.B --help, --version
Show usage or version without invoking ps.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 completed, 1 malformed input records, 2 usage, 3 unavailable input/backend.
.SH SECURITY
Read only. Process details can be sensitive. No root required.
.SH SEE ALSO
ps(1), who(1), session-report(1)
